aily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, jacqueline, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing well, thank you.\n[doctor] good. how\'s your skin doing? i know you\'ve been on the doxycycline for a while now.\n[patient] it\'s better, but it\'s not gone away completely.\n[doctor] okay, so it\'s not fully resolved. have you been using the doxycycline twice a day like i recommended?\n[patient] no, i was using it twice a day, but my pharmacist told me to cut back to once a day since it\'s improving, so i\'ve been using it once a day for about a week now.\n[doctor] okay. and how\'s it been?\n[patient] it hasn\'t gotten worse, but it\'s not getting better either.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been using the sulfacetamide wash?\n[patient] yes, i\'ve been using that twice a day.\n[doctor] and how about drying of the skin?\n[patient] no, it\'s not dry at all. it\'s actually been much improved.\n[doctor] okay. and you\'ve been applying the metrocream after using the sulfacetamide wash?\n[patient] yes, i\'ve been doing that.\n[doctor] okay. have you been using any other lotions or products?\n[patient] no, i\'ve just been using the doxycycline, the sulfacetamide wash, and the metrocream.\n[doctor] okay. have you started the elidel?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t. i was concerned about the side effects.\n[doctor] okay. well, let\'s take a look at your skin. so on your cheeks, i do see a few very faint erythematous papules, some mild redness around and underneath your nostrils. your skin type is probably a four to five.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s talk a little bit about your perioral dermatitis. i know it\'s not fully resolved. i think we should probably get you back on the doxycycline twice a day. have you considered the elidel?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. well, i think we should get you back on the doxycycline twice a day. you can try to do it once a day f