-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning, how are you doing?\n[patient] good morning, i\'m doing alright, thank you. i\'ve been having some abdominal pain.\n[doctor] okay, well, let\'s start with your abdominal pain. what\'s going on with that?\n[patient] well, it\'s been going on for about a week now. it\'s on the right side of my stomach. it\'s really painful. it\'s been bothering me a lot.\n[doctor] okay. have you noticed anything else along with the pain? like nausea or vomiting?\n[patient] yes, i have had some nausea. but no vomiting.\n[doctor] alright. have you had any fevers?\n[patient] i\'ve had a low-grade fever.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any other symptoms like chills or sweating?\n[patient] no, not really.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any other symptoms like headaches or dizziness?\n[patient] no, nothing like that.\n[doctor] alright. i\'m gon na ask you a few more questions. have you had any changes in your bowel habits? like constipation or diarrhea?\n[patient] no, my bowel movements have been normal.\n[doctor] okay. have you noticed any weight loss?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t lost any weight.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any other symptoms like indigestion or heartburn?\n[patient] no, none of that.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any changes in your appetite?\n[patient] no, my appetite\'s been normal.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any other symptoms like joint pain or muscle aches?\n[patient] no, nothing like that.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any recent travel or exposure to anyone who\'s sick?\n[patient] no, i\'ve been staying at home.\n[doctor] okay. all right. let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i\'m gon na listen to your heart and lungs first. your heart sounds normal, nice and regular rate and rhythm. no extra heart sounds like murmurs or clicks. your lungs are clear and equal bilaterally. now, i\'m gon na listen to your abdomen. i do appreciate some slight guarding on the right upper quadrant, but no rebound tenderness. i do appreciate the murphy sign. i\'m gon na press here. does that hurt?\n[patient] yes, that hurts.\n[doctor] okay. i do appreciate that. your abdomen is soft and non-distended. let me check your vital signs. your blood pressure is 128 over 88, respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute, heart rate is 68 beats per minute, oxygen saturation is 99% on room air, and your body temperature is afebrile. let me go ahead and review your blood work. i reviewed the ultrasound from the outside facility, and it shows that you have multiple gallstones in your gallbladder, and there is some mild thickening. the common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm. i do appreciate some inflammation of your gallbladder. have you had any issues with gallstones before?\n[patient] no, i have not.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any family history of gallstones or gallbladder issues?\n[patient] my parents both had cholecystectomies.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any issues with your gallbladder before?\n[patient] no, nothing like that.\n[doctor] okay. so, based on your symptoms, your exam, and your ultrasound, it sounds like you have some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. i do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like your gallbladder is inflamed. this appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since you\'ve been symptomatic. i do believe you need some surgical intervention. i\'m gon na recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. i will explain the surgery, the preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care. the surgery will take approximately 1 to  1.5 hours. you will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. i will also advise you to avoid high fat foods after the surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. all your questions were answered. do you have any questions about the surgery?\n[patient] no, i think you explained everything.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m also gon na recommend that you refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period, but you may return to walking outdoors on solid ground in about 1 week status post-surgery. i also want you to follow up with your primary care physician regarding your impending surgery. we will continue to monitor your diabetes as outlined by either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] do you have any other questions?\n[patient] no, i think i\'m good.\n[doctor] okay. great. do you have a primary care physician?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay. do you have an endocrinologist?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay. i\'ll make sure they\'re aware of the surgery.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] alright. i\'ll have my nurse come in and we\'ll get you checked in for surgery. do you have any other questions or concerns?\n[patient] no, i\'m good.\n[doctor] okay. great. thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'m having some abdominal pain.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m doctor smith. i\'m with united hospital medical group. i\'m one of the surgeons here. i\'m here to see you today. how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] okay. so tell me a little bit about your abdominal pain. how long have you been having it?\n[patient] it\'s been going on for about a week.\n[doctor] okay. have you noticed any nausea or vomiting?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been feeling nauseous.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been able to eat?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t been able to eat much.\n[doctor] o