-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning, how are you doing today?\n[patient] good morning, doctor.\n[doctor] good to see you. so, i understand that you\'re here for right elbow pain. can you tell me a little bit more about what\'s going on?\n[patient] yes, my elbow has been bothering me for about 4 days now. it started hurting after i was working with the pottery wheel.\n[doctor] okay, and you\'ve been using the pottery wheel a lot lately?\n[patient] yes, i\'ve been doing a lot of pottery lately, actually, i was making a bowl for my son.\n[doctor] okay, and how would you rate your pain level right now?\n[patient] it\'s a 7 out of 10.\n[doctor] okay, and is it constant or does it come and go?\n[patient] it\'s constant. it wakes me up at night when i roll over.\n[doctor] okay, and does anything help with the pain?\n[patient] no, i\'ve tried ibuprofen and ice, but it doesn\'t seem to help.\n[doctor] okay, have you had any injuries to that elbow before?\n[patient] no, not that i can remember.\n[doctor] okay, so you\'re having a lot of pain on the medial side of your elbow, and it radiates down into your arm?\n[patient] yes, that\'s right.\n[doctor] okay, and how has this affected your activities?\n[patient] i can\'t use the pottery wheel at all.\n[doctor] okay, how about other activities?\n[patient] it\'s hard to do anything, it\'s really uncomfortable.\n[doctor] okay, have you noticed any swelling or bruising?\n[patient] no, there\'s no bruising or swelling.\n[doctor] okay, so you\'ve been doing a lot of pottery and making a bowl for your son. how has that been going?\n[patient] it\'s been going good. i\'ve been making a lot of pottery, like i said, i was making a bowl for my son. he loves it.\n[doctor] okay, and do you have a history of hypertension?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay, and how are you managing that?\n[patient] i take lisinopril 10 milligrams every day and i check my blood pressure regularly. it\'s usually in the 110s to 130s.\n[doctor] okay, and do you ever miss any doses?\n[patient] no, i try to take it every day.\n[doctor] okay, and do you have a history of diabetes?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay, and how are you managing that?\n[patient] i take metformin 500 milligrams twice a day. sometimes i forget to take it, but i try my best.\n[doctor] okay, and how have your blood sugar levels been?\n[patient] they\'ve been running from 150 to 170.\n[doctor] okay, and how do you exercise?\n[patient] i walk in the morning and in the evening. i walk about 1 to 2 miles a day.\n[doctor] okay, and do you have a sweet tooth?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay, and what do you do for fun?\n[patient] i like working with ceramics and baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n[doctor] okay, so you enjoy baking and ceramics. do you have any other hobbies?\n[patient] yes, i love baking and ceramics.\n[doctor] okay, so let\'s talk a little bit about your review of systems. i know you\'re here for your elbow pain, but can you tell me about any other symptoms you\'re having?\n[patient] no, nothing else.\n[doctor] okay, and how about any rashes or lesions on your skin?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, and any respiratory problems?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, and any pain or discomfort in your heart or chest?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, and any dizziness or lightheadedness?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, and any problems with your vision?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, and any problems with your bowels?\n[patient] no, nothing.\n[doctor] okay, so let\'s talk a little bit about your vital signs. your blood pressure is 120 over 70. your heart rate is 70. your respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute. your oxygen saturation is 100 percent on room air. your body temperature is 97.9 degrees. and your pain level is a 7 out of 10 at its worst. you\'ve gained 5 pounds since your last visit. so, let\'s talk a little bit about the physical exam. i\'m just going to check your heart. i\'m not going to feel for any murmurs or gallops or rubs. your pulses are equal bilaterally. i\'m going to listen to your lungs now. your respiratory effort is normal. you don\'t have any respiratory distress. your lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation. i\'m going to check your skin now. there\'s no rash or lesions. i\'m going to check your musculoskeletal system. let\'s look at your right elbow. there\'s moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. there\'s pain with resisted pronation of your right forearm. there\'s pain with flexion and bending of your right wrist. there\'s no bruising or swelling. there\'s no discoloration or lesions. so, let\'s talk a little bit about the results. i reviewed your x-ray of your right elbow today and there\'s no evidence of any fractures. there are no other abnormalities noted. so, based on your symptoms and your exam, it sounds like you have medial epicondylitis, which is inflammation of the tendons on the inside of your elbow. this is probably caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm. i\'m going to recommend that you wear a sling on your right arm while you\'re awake to provide some increased support. i\'m also going to have you ice your right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day. you can take ibuprofen 600 milligrams every 6 hours with food for a week. i\'m also going to have you hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal. if you continue to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit. i\'m also going to encourage you to check and record your blood sugars every day. i\'m going to increase your metformin to 1000 milligrams twice a day. it\'s very important that you take your medication on a regular basis twice a day. i\'m also going to order a hemoglobin a1c test for a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels. and i\'m also going to refer you to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps you can take to manage your diabetes. for your hypertension, you\'re currently controlled with medication and exercise, so i\'m going to continue you on lisinopril 10 milligrams. i also encourage you to continue with your walking routine. so, all your questions were answered. do you have any other questions for me?\n[patient] no, i think that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay, great. so, i\'ll see you back in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin a1c test and discuss your progress. do you have any other questions for me?\n[patient] no, that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay, great. take care. see you in 2 weeks.\n[patient] thank you.\n[doctor] you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, teresa, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing well, thank you.\n[doctor] good. so, you\'re here today for some right elbow pain. how long have you been having that?\n[patient] four days.\n[doctor] okay. and how would you rate the pain, out of ten?\n[patient] it\'s a seven.\n[doctor] okay. and what\'s causing you to come in today?\n[patient] i\'ve been doing a lot of pottery. i\'m trying to make a bowl for my son.\n[doctor] okay. and what\'s happening when you\'re doing that, is the pain happening while you\'re doing that?\n[patient] yes, it\'s happening when i\'m doing that.\n[doctor] okay. and is there any specific part of the pottery process that\'s causing it?\n[patient] no, i just feel it when i\'m working with the clay.\n[doctor] okay. and are you able to work on it at all?\n[patient] no, i\'m not able to work on it.\n[doctor] okay. and what kind of pain are you having?\n[patient] it\'s just kind of a sharp pain in the back of my elbow.\n[doctor] okay. and is it just the elbow or does it go down into your arm?\n[patient] it goes down into my arm.\n[doctor] okay. and when you say down into your arm, does it go all the way to your hand or does it just go down to your wrist?\n[patient] it goes down to my wrist.\n[doctor] okay. and is it more on the top or the bottom of your arm?\n[patient] it\'s on the top of my arm.\n[doctor] okay. and are you using ice or ibuprofen at all?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and have you ever injured your elbow before?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and are you working on this pottery wheel a lot more than you normally do?\n[patient] yes, i have been using it a lot more.\n[doctor] okay. and how often do you normally use it?\n[patient] i would say once a week.\n[doctor] okay. and is this the first time you\'ve used it this much?\n[patient] yes, it is.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been doing anything else different in your daily routine?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?\n[patient] yes, i have high blood pressure and type two diabetes.\n[doctor] okay. and are you on any medications for those?\n[patient] yes, i\'m on lisinopril and metformin.\n[doctor] okay. and are you taking those regularly?\n[patient] i try to.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any other questions?\n[patient] no, i don\'t.\n[doctor] okay. well, let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i\'m gon na check your blood pressure. it\'s one twenty over seventy. your heart rate is one thirty-five and regular. your lungs are clear bilaterally. your skin exam is normal. i\'m gon na go ahead and examine your right elbow. and i\'m gon na press right here on the medial epicondyle. and you do have some tenderness on the medial epicondyle. you have pain with resisted pronation of your right forearm. and you also have pain with flexion and bending of your right wrist. so i\'m gon na go ahead and order an x-ray of your right elbow. and that will help us determine what\'s going on with your elbow pain.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so the x-ray of your right elbow was reviewed today. and it demonstrates no evidence of any fractures. there are no other abnormalities noted. so based on your examination and radiographic findings, i\'m gon na tell you that your elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis. and that\'s basically a strain of the tendons in your elbow. and that\'s most likely caused by overuse and damage to those tendons. i\'m gon na recommend that we treat you conservatively. i\'m gon na have you wear a sling on your right arm while you\'re awake to provide a little bit more support. i\'m gon na have you ice your elbow for twenty minutes, three times a day. you can take ibuprofen six hundred milligrams every six hours with food. and that\'s gon na be for a week. i\'m gon na have you hold off on creating pottery for the next two weeks to allow your arm to heal. if you continue to have elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit. i\'m gon na encourage you to check and record your blood sugars every day. i\'m gon na increase your metformin to one thousand milligrams twice a day. you\'re gon na continue to take your lisinopril. you\'re gon na continue with your walking routine. i\'m gon na order a hemoglobin a1c test to get a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels. and i\'m gon na encourage you to continue with your regular blood sugar checks. i\'m gon na have you come back and see me in two weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin a1c test and discuss your progress. and if you have any other questions, feel free to ask.\n[patient] okay. thank you.\n[doctor] you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] alright teresa i see here in your chart that you seem to be experiencing some elbow pain could you tell me where it hurts\n[patient] yeah so it\'s hurting on the inside of my elbow\n[doctor] mm-hmm\n[patient] and when i i i\'ve been doing some pottery i\'ve been creating you know i have a pottery wheel and i\'ve been i love my pottery and for my son\'s birthday i\'ve been making a a ceramic bowl and i\'m gon na paint it and i have to do some real full decorations but i\'ve really been working on it a lot and my gosh this is hurting and i ca n\'t work on it right now because it\'s hurting so much\n[doctor] i am so sorry so i mean that\'s really cool that you do parttery it\'s something that i\'ve wanted to pick up you know but i do n\'t know that i have those skills yet does the pain radiate down your arm or up your shoulder or anything\n[patie