 He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi dennis how are you doing today\n[patient] i\'m doing okay\n[doctor] good to see you again dennis i\'m dr martin i\'m here for the evaluation of your right heel pain\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] so tell me a little bit about what happened\n[patient] okay so i\'m training for a triathlon and i\'ve been doing a lot of running and biking and swimming and my heel has been bothering me for about the last 10 days\n[doctor] okay and where exactly is the pain located\n[patient] so it\'s on the back of my heel\n[doctor] okay and does it radiate anywhere else\n[patient] it radiates up into my calf\n[doctor] okay and is it worse at any particular times\n[patient] it\'s worse when i\'m working out and when i first get up in the morning\n[doctor] okay and does it come back if you sit for a long period of time and then get up\n[patient] yeah it comes back\n[doctor] okay alright so you\'re doing a triathlon training\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] how many kilometers do you run in the morning\n[patient] i run 10 kilometers\n[doctor] okay and what do you do at lunchtime\n[patient] at lunchtime i try to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour\n[doctor] okay and in the evening\n[patient] i go to the aquatic center and i swim for 30 minutes to an hour\n[doctor] okay and how often do you do this\n[patient] i run five days a week\n[doctor] okay and do you alternate between a long run and a long bike ride on the weekends\n[patient] yeah i do\n[doctor] okay and do you take any days off from your exercises\n[patient] i take one day off\n[doctor] okay and do you stretch at all\n[patient] no i do not\n[doctor] okay and have you noticed any pain in your left leg\n[patient] no i have not\n[doctor] okay alright so let\'s go ahead and do a physical exam of your right heel\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i\'m going to press here on the back of your heel and it\'s painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and then i\'m going to press here on the top of your heel and that\'s also painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and then i\'m going to press here on the bottom of your heel and that\'s also painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m going to press here on the top of your foot and that\'s also painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and i\'m going to press here on the bottom of your foot and that\'s not painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m going to press here on the back of your heel and it\'s painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and then i\'m going to squeeze your heel and that is also painful\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and then i\'m going to push on the b