 fatigue. She states that she has been wearing her regular shoes.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. She states that her blood sugars are running better than they were; however, they are still in the 200\'s. Her last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nSkin: Reports right foot ulcer and drainage.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo fever noted. Vitals look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right foot: There is a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I do see some yellow slough that is present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. I do not see necrosis. No odor. I do not appreciate any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot today reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight diabetic foot ulcer.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that we obtain an ankle brachial index to determine her blood supply to see if we can heal the wound. We discussed doing a debridement in office today, which she agreed to. I have also recommended that the patient be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I have also prescribed the patient collagenase ointment to apply to her wound 1 time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing. I advised the patient that this should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to check on her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning christina how are you\n[patient] good morning\n[doctor] so i\'m going to go ahead and review your vitals here and you\'re looking good so far your blood pressure is 120 over 70 your heart rate is 78 and your respiratory rate is 16 and your oxygen saturation is 98 percent on room air so i\'m going to go ahead and review your history now so you\'re here for a right foot ulcer so tell me a little bit about that\n[patient] yes i have a ulcer on my right foot\n[doctor] okay so can you tell me how long you\'ve had this\n[patient] about a year\n[doctor] okay and what happened\n[patient] i was walking and i felt like i got a blister and then it turned red and i picked at it and it just never got better\n[doctor] okay and have you had any treatment for it\n[patient] yes i went to my primary care doctor and she gave me some antibiotics\n[doctor] okay and did those help\n[patient] yes it did but it didn\'t completely get rid of it\n[doctor] okay and when was the last time you were on antibiotics\n[patient] about five months ago\n[doctor] okay and how are you feeling right now\n[patient] i\'m okay i\'m just tired and it\'s really draining\n[doctor] okay and do you have any pain\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and are you able to walk\n[patient] yes i\'m wearing my regular shoes\n[doctor] okay and do you have any numbness or tingling in your foot\n[patient] yes i have a lot of numbness\n[doctor] okay and have you had that numbness for a while\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and what type of diabetes do you have\n[patient] type two\n[doctor] okay and what\'s your hemoglobin a1c\n[patient] it was ten last time i checked\n[doctor] okay and are you having a problem with your blood sugars\n[patient] no i\'ve been running a little better\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] but they\'re still in the two hundreds\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other medical conditions\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay so let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam of your foot so i\'m gon na look at your right foot here and i do see a one centimeter by two centimeter wound on the dorsal aspect of your lateral right foot just proximal to the fifth mtp joint i do see some yellow slough that\'s present with minimal granulation tissue there\'s no surrounding erythema or cellulitis there\'s no evidence of fluid collection i do not see necrosis no odor no bony exposure no pain to palpation on exam of the foot and i do appreciate decreased sensation of your foot so i\'m gon na order an ankle brachial index to see if we can determine your blood supply to see if we can heal this wound i\'m gon na go ahead and do a debridement in the office today and i\'m gon na place you in a surgical shoe to provide increased support i\'m gon na prescribe you collagenase ointment to apply to your wound one time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing and you should continue that until we see the ulcer decrease in size so i want you to follow up with me in two weeks to check on your progress and we\'ll go from there okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay so do you have any questions for me\n[patient] no }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] christina cooper is a pleasant seventy seven year old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a non healing right foot ulcer. she states that the onset of her wound began one year ago with no specific injury. she states that it seemed like a blister and she picked at it where it then turned red. the patient was seen by her primary care physician and was prescribed keflex. she states that the antibiotics improved her symptoms however they did not resolve them. the patient adds that she was last on keflex five months ago. she denies any pain however she reports decreased sensation in her foot. she states that she has been wearing her regular shoes. she has type two diabetes with neuropathy. she states that her blood sugars are running better than they were however they are still in the two hundreds. her last hemoglobin a one c was ten. how are you doing today?\n[patient] i\'m doing well. i\'m still having some issues with my foot.\n[doctor] okay. so tell me about your foot.\n[patient] it\'s just that my foot is still hurting and i have a big sore on the side of my foot.\n[doctor] okay. and how long have you had the sore?\n[patient] about a year.\n[doctor] okay. and how did it