tal signs look good with blood pressure of 127/80.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort: Normal respiratory effort.\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Slight, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLab results are reviewed and show stable a creatinine level.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing very well and has been compliant with all medications. A slight 3/6 ejection murmur was heard on exam, however, this is stable and has been present in the past. He is experiencing 1+ non-pitting edema, but this seems to be medication related.\n\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Patient was advised that his stents will remain in place permanently and that they typically do not require replacement. We discussed it is common to experience dizziness when standing quickly and that this should resolve once he is adjusted to his medications. We also discussed that cardiac rehab will be beneficial in helping him become more active and confident with exercising. He was advised to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion as this would need further testing such as a stress test.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab was provided. A refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day and Crestor 40 mg once a day was also provided. He is also advised to continue aspirin daily long term as well as Brilinta for the next 6 months.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient\'s most recent creatinine levels are stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of following up with nephrology.\n- Medical Treatment: He will follow up with his nephrologist, Dr. Miller.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports most recent hemoglobin A1c was 16.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diabetes management and how plays an important role in his coronary artery disease. He was advised that his hemoglobin A1c goal will be 6.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat hemoglobin A1c was ordered today. A dietitian referral for diabetes education was also provided. We will contact his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen.\n\n4. Mild depression:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports feeling depressed and having a hard time adjusting since having his heart attack. He mentions he is also lonely and does not have a support system.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to psychiatry was provided today.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing alright, i\'m just happy to be here.\n[doctor] great. so, so i know it\'s been six months since your heart attack, how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'m feeling a little better. i\'ve been doing my physical therapy and stuff like that.\n[doctor] okay. and you\'re doing your physical therapy, that\'s great. have you noticed any dizziness when you stand up quickly?\n[patient] yeah, sometimes.\n[doctor] okay. and any headaches?\n[patient] yeah, some headaches.\n[doctor] okay. and how about muscle aches?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. any other symptoms?\n[patient] no, i just feel really tired a lot.\n[doctor] okay. well, let\'s start with your heart attack. so, i know you had a heart attack six months ago. and i know you had a stent put in your heart. how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] okay. and i know you\'re from the west coast, you\'re a san francisco 49ers fan, and you live with your dog. how\'s the dog doing?\n[patient] yeah, my dog is doing good.\n[doctor] okay, that\'s good. so, i know you\'re taking aspirin, brilinta, fish oil, crestor, and metoprolol. how are you doing with those?\n[patient] yeah, i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] okay. and any issues with the medications?\n[patient] no, i\'m doing fine.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s go over your review of systems. so, any muscle aches, any joint pain?\n[patient] no, none of that.\n[doctor] okay. any neurological symptoms, like dizziness or headaches?\n[patient] yeah, some dizziness and some headaches.\n[doctor] okay. any depression or anxiety?\n[patient] yeah, i\'m feeling a little depressed.\n[doctor] okay. any suicidal or homicidal thoughts?\n[patient] no, none of that.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s look at your vital signs. your blood pressure is 127 over 80, which is good. let\'s do a quick physical exam. so, i\'m just going to listen to your heart and lungs. i\'m hearing a slight three over six systolic ejection murmur here at the left base. does that bother you?\n[patient] no, no, that\'s okay.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m also seeing a one plus non-pitting edema in your legs. does that mean anything to you?\n[patient] no, what is that?\n[doctor] that\'s just fluid in your legs. it\'s probably from the blood pressure medicine. but that\'s okay. let\'s go over your lab results. your creatinine levels are stable. that\'s good. so, let\'s talk about your heart attack. so, your stents are in place and they\'re going to stay there permanently. they usually don\'t need to be replaced.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] and you\'re doing well with your medications. i\'m glad you\'re taking them. i know it\'s a lot to take, but it\'s very important. so, i know you had a heart attack. it\'s common to feel a little dizzy when you stand up quickly. does that happen?\n[patient] yeah, yeah, that does.\n[doctor] okay. and does it go away?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and any chest pain or shortness of breath when you\'re exerting yourself?\n[patient] no, no chest pain.\n[doctor] okay. so, i\'m glad to hear that. i think you should continue to watch for that, though. if you have any of those symptoms, we\'ll need to do a stress test. so, i know you\'re doing physical therapy. have you been able to do anything else to get more active?\n[patient] no, i\'ve been doing a little bit of walking.\n[doctor] okay. well, i think you should definitely try to get more active. i\'m going to refer you to cardiac rehab. they can help you become more active and confident with exercising. and then, we\'ll get you on a dietitian for diabetes education. you\'ve had a heart attack, so that\'s very important. so, let\'s talk about your diabetes. so, i know you had a hemoglobin a1c of 16 last time. what\'s your diet like?\n[patient] i\'m just eating whatever i want.\n[doctor] okay. well, we need to get you on a better diet. we\'re going to order another hemoglobin a1c today. your goal is a six. so, we\'ll get you on a dietitian and we\'ll get you on a diet that will help you get that hemoglobin a1c down. and then, we\'ll get you on insulin if we need to. but i\'m going to hold off on that for now. so, let\'s talk about your kidney function. your creatinine levels are stable, which is good. we\'ll continue to follow up with nephrology, okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. and then, let\'s talk about your depression. how are you doing with that?\n[patient] i\'m feeling a little down.\n[doctor] okay. i know it\'s hard to adjust. it\'s been six months since your heart attack. it\'s a big change. but i\'m going to refer you to psychiatry. they can help you with that. and then, let\'s talk about your medications. so, we\'re going to continue you on the metoprolol 50 milligrams once a day and the crestor 40 milligrams once a day. you\'ll continue to take aspirin daily long term. and the brilinta, you\'ll take that for the next six months. so, do you have any questions?\n[patient] no, no.\n[doctor] okay. well, do you agree with the plan?\n[patient] yes, i do.\n[doctor] okay. great. i\'ll have my nurse come in and we\'ll get you set up with all the referrals and prescriptions. do you have any other questions for me?\n[patient] no, i think that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay. well, it was good seeing you. take care.\n[patient] thank you.\n[doctor] bye. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] all right, so i know it\'s been a little while since we last spoke, but i\'m glad you\'re here today. i know you\'ve had a lot going on.\n[patient] yeah, i have, i\'ve been really struggling since the heart attack, i just feel like i\'m just not the same person anymore.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m so sorry to hear that. so, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. can you tell me about that?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been having a lot of chest pain, i\'ve been having shortness of breath, i\'ve been having dizziness, i\'ve been having headaches, i\'ve been having a lot of anxiety and depression.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m sorry to hear that. um, i know you\'ve been having some dizziness. are you dizzy when you stand up?\n[patient] yeah, i\'m really dizzy when i stand up, and i\'m really dizzy when i\'m walking around.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. are you having chest pain when you\'re walking?\n[patient] yeah, i\'m having chest pain when i\'m walking, i\'m having chest pain when i\'m sitting, i\'m having chest pain when i\'m lying down, it\'s just constant.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some shortness of breath. do you have shortness of breath when you\'re walking up the stairs?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have shortness of breath when i\'m walking up the stairs, but i do have shortness of breath when i\'m walking around the block.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some headaches. do you have headaches when you\'re lying down?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have headaches when i\'m lying down, but i do have headaches when i\'m sitting up.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some anxiety and depression. are you having anxiety and depression when you\'re lying down?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have anxiety and depression when i\'m lying down, but i do have anxiety and depression when i\'m sitting up, and i do have anxiety and depression when i\'m standing up.\n[doctor] okay. um, i\'m sorry to hear that. um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve noticed a lot of symptoms. i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my legs, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my arms, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my chest, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my back, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my stomach, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my head, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my neck.\n[doctor] okay. um, have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve noticed a lot of other symptoms. i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my legs, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my ankles, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my feet, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my hands, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my fingers, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my face, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my eyes, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my tongue.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve noticed a lot of other symptoms. i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my back, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my stomach, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my head, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my neck, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my arms, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my legs, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my chest.\n[doctor] okay. um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, i know you\'ve been having some shortness of breath. um, i know you\'ve been having some dizziness. um, i know you\'ve been having some headaches. um, i know you\'ve been having some anxiety and depression. um, i know you\'ve been having some pain in your back and stomach. um, i know you\'ve been having some pain in your head and neck. um, i know you\'ve been having some pain in your arms and legs. um, i know you\'ve been having some pain in your chest. um, i know you\'ve been having some swelling in your legs, ankles, and feet. um, i know you\'ve been having some swelling in your hands, fingers, and face. um, i know you\'ve been having some swelling in your eyes and tongue. um, i\'m sorry to hear that. um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you had any chest pain in the past?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve had chest pain in the past, but i\'ve been having a lot of chest pain recently.\n[doctor] okay. um, um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you had any chest pain when you\'re lying down?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have chest pain when i\'m lying down.\n[doctor] okay. um, um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you had any chest pain when you\'re walking up the stairs?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have chest pain when i\'m walking up the stairs.\n[doctor] okay. um, um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you had any chest pain when you\'re walking around the block?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have chest pain when i\'m walking around the block.\n[doctor] okay. um, um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve noticed a lot of symptoms. i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my legs, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my arms, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my chest, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my back, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my stomach, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my head, i\'ve noticed a lot of pain in my neck.\n[doctor] okay. um, um, um, i know you\'ve been having some chest pain. um, have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve noticed a lot of other symptoms. i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my legs, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my ankles, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling in my feet, i\'ve noticed a lot of swelling