epatomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nRESULTS\n\nRecent cholesterol level is reviewed and shows patient is elevated at 210.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Elevated cholesterol.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s previous tests are consistent with hypercholesterolemia.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends children between the ages of 9 to 11 years old and again between 17 to 20 years old be screened for their cholesterol. We also discussed that studies link high cholesterol to pre-mature heart attacks. I advised the patient that genetic testing can be done to see if the problem is genetic or a secondary cause. They were also advised to follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars, as well as remain active.\n- Medical Treatment: Full cholesterol panel ordered. Hemoglobin A1c, CMP, thyroid study, and liver panel were also ordered. Genetic testing ordered. Referral to nutritionist provided.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow-up in 3 to 6 months after meeting the nutritionist for another cholesterol level.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning, how are you?\n[patient] good morning, doctor.\n[doctor] so, i understand you\'re here today because of your cholesterol levels. how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'ve been feeling okay, i just wanted to get a check up.\n[doctor] alright, so you\'re here for your annual check up. so, i reviewed your vital signs and your blood pressure looks good. so, tell me a little bit about your family history. have you had any family members who have had heart attacks or any other cardiovascular issues?\n[patient] well, my mom and dad both smoked, but i\'ve never smoked.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any other family members who\'ve had heart attacks or any other cardiovascular issues?\n[patient] well, my cousin died of a heart attack in his 40\'s.\n[doctor] okay, i\'m sorry to hear that. but does anyone else in your family have high cholesterol?\n[patient] no, not that i\'m aware of.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any other medical conditions like diabetes or high blood pressure?\n[patient] no, i\'ve never had any of those.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s talk a bit about your lifestyle. do you enjoy any outdoor activities?\n[patient] yes, i like riding my bike on trails.\n[doctor] that sounds nice. how often do you ride?\n[patient] whenever i can.\n[doctor] okay. so, have you noticed any xanthomas on your skin?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s do a quick physical exam. i\'m just going to listen to your heart and lungs. and i\'m going to feel your neck and abdomen. so, your heart sounds good, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. your lungs are clear bilaterally. your neck is supple, no thyromegaly. and your abdomen is soft, no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. so, let\'s talk a bit about your diet. do you follow a specific diet?\n[patient] i try to eat pretty healthy, but i like pizza and ice cream.\n[doctor] okay, yeah, it\'s hard to resist those. and do you exercise regularly?\n[patient] i try to ride my bike and go for walks.\n[doctor] that\'s good. so, i reviewed your recent cholesterol level and it shows that you\'re elevated at 210. so, let\'s talk a bit about what this means. your cholesterol is elevated, but we need to check a few more things to see what\'s causing it. i\'m going to order a full cholesterol panel, a hemoglobin a1c, a cmp, a thyroid study, and a liver panel. we\'ll also do genetic testing to see if it\'s a genetic issue or if there\'s another cause. we\'ll also refer you to a nutritionist to help you with your diet. so, let\'s talk a bit about what we\'re going to do. i know you\'re probably feeling a bit concerned, but we\'re going to get this taken care of. so, let\'s start with your diet. we\'re going to have you follow a balanced diet with a variety of foods that are low in saturated fat and sugars. and we\'ll also have you try to stay active, like you\'re doing now with your bike rides. so, i know it\'s a lot to take in, but let\'s take it one step at a time. does that sound good?\n[patient] yes, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. and then we\'ll follow up in about three to six months to see how you\'re doing. does that sound good?\n[patient] yes, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay, great. do you have any questions for me?\n[patient] no, i think you\'ve covered everything.\n[doctor] okay. alright, well, i\'m going to have my nurse come in and get you set up for the blood work and the genetic testing. and then you\'ll go see the nutritionist and we\'ll see you back here in about three to six months.\n[patient] okay, sounds good.\n[doctor] alright, thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, dr. good to see you. how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing well. how are you?\n[doctor] i\'m doing well. so, i know you\'re here for a routine checkup. how can i help you today?\n[patient] i\'m having some high cholesterol. i\'m a little concerned about it.\n[doctor] okay. um, tell me a little bit about your high cholesterol. when was the last time you had your cholesterol checked?\n[patient] i\'m not really sure.\n[doctor] okay. um, what was the reading?\n[patient] i think it was like two hundred and ten.\n[doctor] okay. and when was that?\n[patient] um, i\'m not really sure. i\'ve been trying to get it checked out. i know i need to get it checked out.\n[doctor] okay. um, well, i\'m gon na go ahead and order that. so, tell me a little bit about your diet. do you have any dietary restrictions?\n[patient] not really. i eat pretty much anything.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any problems with your weight?\n[patient] i\'m trying to lose a little bit of weight.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any problems with your heart or your blood pressure?\n[patient] no, i don\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any