ear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: No masses or tenderness. Soft, no guarding.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in the ankles. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG was obtained and reviewed in office today and is unremarkable. Hemoglobin A1c: 7.0\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Considering her history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect her chest pain is likely unstable angina. Her recent EKG was unremarkable and there are no other signs of heart attack, but I am worried she may have some sort of a blockage causing her pain.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. I advised her to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after approximately 5 minutes.\n- Medical Treatment: We will have her start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily, for which I have sent in a prescription today. I also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. Finally, I am going to have her follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: She appears to be doing well on her current regimen.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to keep her appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments and have her follow up in 2 weeks. In the meantime, I want her to continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in the patient\'s chart.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to monitor her blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report her readings to me via MyChart. This should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to her new medications. We discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide how this will hopefully reduce some of her swelling seen on physical exam. All of her questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to have her start hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hey, good morning, how are you?\n[patient] good morning, doctor.\n[doctor] how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay, i\'ve been having some chest pain.\n[doctor] chest pain, have you been having any chest pain?\n[patient] yes, i have.\n[doctor] okay. have you had this before?\n[patient] no, it\'s new.\n[doctor] okay. okay. have you had any chest pain before?\n[patient] no, i\'ve never had any chest pain before.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and how long has it been going on?\n[patient] for about a week.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and it\'s been happening every day?\n[patient] yeah, pretty much every day.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and how would you describe the pain?\n[patient] it\'s like a tightness in my chest.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and is it constant or does it come and go?\n[patient] it\'s mostly constant.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it get worse when you\'re doing anything?\n[patient] when i\'m doing anything that\'s strenuous.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. and how would you describe the pain when you\'re doing something strenuous?\n[patient] it gets a lot worse.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. and does it get better when you stop?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. and have you had any other symptoms like sweating, shortness of breath, nausea or vomiting?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had any of those symptoms.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. and have you had any diarrhea or abdominal pain?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had any of those symptoms either.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. all right. so let\'s go ahead and do a physical exam. so i\'m just gon na listen to your heart and lungs and your abdomen and your neck.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so your heart sounds good. you have a little bit of a systolic ejection murmur, but that\'s not uncommon. your lungs are clear. your abdomen is soft, no masses or tenderness. and your neck is okay. no swelling or carotid bruits.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so i\'m gon na order an ekg. and i\'m gon na order a hemoglobin a1c. and i\'m gon na have you start taking baby aspirin. we\'ll get you set up with a prescription for that. and i\'m gon na have you start taking nitroglycerin, up to three tablets every five minutes as needed. and if that doesn\'t help, we\'re gon na send you to the emergency room.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na put you on a low dose of hydrochlorothiazide. this will help with the swelling that i see in your ankles. and i\'m gon na have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so we need to get your hemoglobin a1c checked. i\'m gon na order that today. and i want you to follow up in two weeks. and in the meantime, continue to take your metformin, 500 milligrams twice a day. and i want you to keep your diet modified.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na have you follow up with ophthalmology for your diabetic retinopathy.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. and i want you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next two weeks and report those to me via mychart. this will help us determine if we need to make any adjustments to your new medications.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. all right. do you have any questions for me?\n[patient] no, i think you\'ve covered everything.\n[doctor] okay. all right. you take care.\n[patient] thank you.\n[doctor] you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay, i\'m just having some chest pain on exertion.\n[doctor] okay. so, um, i understand that you have some chest pain on exertion. um, when you say on exertion, do you mean when you\'re walking or running or lifting?\n[patient] um, it\'s like when i\'m walking up the stairs.\n[doctor] okay. and when you say you\'re walking up the stairs, do you mean like, you\'re walking up the stairs at home or like at work?\n[patient] um, just at home.\n[doctor] okay. and when you say you\'re having chest pain, what does that feel like? is it like a tightness or a heaviness or a burning sensation?\n[patient] it\'s just a tightness.\n[doctor] okay. and does it go to your arm or your jaw or your back?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and when did this start?\n[patient] um, a few days ago.\n[doctor] okay. and how many days ago?\n[patient] um, i\'m not sure.\n[doctor] okay. um, and how long does the pain last?\n[patient] um, it\'s like, it just goes away when i stop.\n[doctor] okay. and does it come back?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and when you say you stop, do you mean you stop walking?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and how long does the pain last after you stop?\n[patient] um, i don\'t know, just a few minutes.\n[doctor] okay. okay. um, and have you had any chest pain before?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and how about shortness of breath? do you have any shortness of breath?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and any nausea or vomiting or diarrhea?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any chest pain on lying down?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any chest pain when you\'re sitting?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and do you have a family history of heart disease?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been diagnosed with high blood pressure?\n[patient] i haven\'t been diagnosed with high blood pressure, but my blood pressures have been a little high.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have diabetes?\n[patient] yes, i have type two diabetes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and what kind of medication are you taking for your diabetes?\n[patient] um, metformin.\n[doctor] okay. and how much do you take?\n[patient] 500 milligrams twice a day.\n[doctor] okay. and any other medications?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any other chest pain before?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, do you have any other medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, and any other medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. um, so let\'s do a quick physical exam. so, let me listen to your heart. i\'m gon na listen to your heart and lungs. okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] so, i\'m gon na listen to your heart. your heart is normal rate and rhythm, and i do hear a two over six systolic ejection murmur. your lungs are clear bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your neck is normal, no jugular venous distension or swelling, no carotid bruits. your abdomen is soft and nontender. your examination of your extremities shows one plus nonpitting edema in your ankles, no calf tenderness. so, let\'s talk about your chest pain. um, i think that you might be having some unstable angina, which is a blockage in one of your arteries that\'s causing the pain. um, i know you have a history of diabetes and you\'ve had high blood pressures in the past. um, i know that you have a family history of heart disease. um, so i\'m gon na put you on some medications. i\'m gon na put you on baby aspirin at 81 milligrams daily. i\'m gon na give you a prescription for nitroglycerin, three tablets every five minutes as needed, but i want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes. um, i\'m also gon na have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation. um, i\'m gon na have you follow up in two weeks. um, let\'s talk about your diabetes. um, you\'re doing well on your current regimen. um, i want you to keep your appointment with ophthalmology for the evaluation of diabetic retinopathy. um, i\'m gon na put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin a1c to check for any needed medication adjustments, and have you follow up in two weeks. um, i want you to continue with metformin 500 milligrams twice a day, and dietary modifications. um, let\'s talk about your high blood pressure. um, you have had high blood pressures in the past. um, i\'m gon na have you start on hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose. um, i want you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next two weeks, and report those to me via mychart. this should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. um, do you have any questions?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay.\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. go ahead.\n[patient] so, i have a few questions. um, so, um, do you think this is like a heart attack or something?\n[doctor] um, i think it\'s more like a blockage in one of your arteries that\'s causing the pain. um, i don\'t think it\'s a heart attack, but i do want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.\n[patient] okay. and then, um, what\'s the blockage? is it like, is it a clogged artery or?\n[doctor] it\'s a blockage in one of your arteries.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. and what is the medication that you prescribed, uh, for the chest pain?\n[doctor] um, i prescribed you some nitroglycerin, and you can take three tablets every five minutes as needed, but i want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.\n[patient] okay. and then, um, um, how will i know if it\'s a heart attack? like, what are the signs?\n[doctor] um, if you\'re having chest pain, and you\'re having shortness of breath, and you\'re having nausea, and you\'re vomiting, and you\'re having diarrhea, then that\'s a heart attack. um, but if you\'re just having chest pain and you\'re not having any of those other symptoms, then it\'s probably a blockage in one of your arteries.\n[patient] okay. and then, um, um, i have diabetes, and i have a family history of heart disease, so, um, what, like, what is the likelihood of me having a heart attack?\n[doctor] um, i think you\'re at a higher risk for having a heart attack because you have a family history of heart disease, and you have diabetes. um, and you\'ve had high blood pressures in the past. um, i think you\'re at a higher risk, but i don\'t think it\'s a high risk. um, i think it\'s more like a moderate risk. um, but i do want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.\n[patient] okay. and then, um, how do i get the medication?\n[doctor] um, i\'ll give you a prescription, and you can pick it up at the pharmacy. um, you can also call the pharmacy and have it delivered to your home.\n[patient] okay. and then, um, um, how much does it cost?\n[doctor] um, i\'m gon na give you a prescription, and you can go to the pharmacy, and you can pick it up, and they\'ll give you the price. um, if you have insurance, it\'ll probably be covered, but if you don\'t have insurance, it\'ll probably be around 50 dollars.\n[patient] okay. okay. thank you so much.\n[doctor] okay. you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] so elizabeth is a 53 -year-old female today complaining of chest pain and she has a past medical history of diabetes and high blood pressure so elizabeth tell me what\'s going on with your with your chest pain\n[patient] i do n\'t know every time i start walking i\'m just having this kind of pressure and chest pain in my chest\n[doctor] okay are you having it now or is it gone right now\n[patient] right now it\'s not there\n[doctor] okay and how long have you