sk of elevated glucose levels if the patient were diabetic.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, kyle. how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing alright. how are you?\n[doctor] i\'m doing well. so, i see that you\'re here for low back pain. can you tell me a little bit about that?\n[patient] yeah, so i\'ve been having some back pain for about two weeks now. it started when i was lifting a box of books and i felt a pop. and then it just started to hurt and it\'s been getting worse since then.\n[doctor] okay, so it\'s been about two weeks and it\'s been getting worse. have you had any pain with sitting?\n[patient] no, i have not.\n[doctor] okay. have you been doing anything new or different in your daily activities that might have caused the pain?\n[patient] i have a sedentary job, so i\'m not really doing anything new. i just started lifting some boxes and it just hurt.\n[doctor] okay. and you said it felt like a pop when you were lifting the box. is the pain sharp or is it more of an ache?\n[patient] it\'s more of an ache.\n[doctor] okay. and is it just in the low back or is it radiating down your leg?\n[patient] it\'s radiating down my left leg.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt with sitting?\n[patient] no, it does not.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been taking any pain medications for it?\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen and tylenol.\n[doctor] okay. does that help?\n[patient] a little bit.\n[doctor] okay. have you seen a doctor for this before?\n[patient] no, i have not.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any numbness or tingling in your legs?\n[patient] no, i have not.\n[doctor] okay. any weakness in your legs?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. any loss of bladder or bowel control?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. do you feel like it\'s affecting your daily activities?\n[patient] no, it\'s not affecting my job, but i feel like i\'m not as active as i used to be.\n[patient] i try to exercise a few times a week, but i\'m not very consistent.\n[doctor] okay. have you ever had any injuries or trauma to your back?\n[patient] no, i played soccer in the midfield position.\n[doctor] okay. and does your family have any history of back problems?\n[patient] my father has back pain.\n[doctor] okay. any other medical problems?\n[patient] no, i do not have any other medical problems.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i\'m gon na check your vital signs. your blood pressure is 130 over 70, and your respiratory rate is 18. let\'s start with your cardiovascular exam. i don\'t appreciate any murmurs, rubs, or gallops. let\'s move on to your respiratory exam. your respiratory effort is normal and there\'s no respiratory distress. your lungs are clear to auscultation, bilaterally. let\'s move on to your back exam. i don\'t appreciate any trauma or deformity. i do appreciate some midline tenderness at the l4-5 disc space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. there\'s pain with lumbar flexion and a positive supine straight leg test. on your neurological exam, i do appreciate normal sensation. your patellar and achilles reflexes are symmetrical. let\'s review your results. your mri of the lumbar spine shows disc desiccation and a disc bulge with a paracentral disc herniation resulting in moderate right neural foraminal stenosis. based on your examination and your mri findings, it sounds like you have a herniated disc with radiculopathy. i want to discuss some treatment options with you. have you had any surgery before?\n[patient] no, i have not.\n[doctor] okay. based on your exam and your mri findings, i think we should start with some conservative treatment. i\'m gon na recommend a corticosteroid injection into your lumbar spine today. the injection will help reduce the swelling and inflammation around the disc. this will hopefully help with the pain and the numbness in your leg. are you willing to try the injection?\n[patient] yes, i am.\n[doctor] okay. before we proceed with the injection, i want to let you know that there are some risks associated with it. there\'s a risk of bleeding, infection, nerve damage, blood vessel damage, reaction to the medication, including skin changes, swelling, and also the risk of elevated glucose levels if you were diabetic. are you diabetic?\n[patient] no, i am not.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any other medical problems that i should be aware of?\n[patient] no, i do not.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s go ahead and proceed with the injection. i\'ll go ahead and clean the area with some alcohol and then administer the injection. you may feel a pinch when i inject the medication. it should take about 10 minutes to complete. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no, i do not.\n[doctor] okay. i\'ll be back in a few minutes to check on you. do you have any other questions or concerns?\n[patient] no, i do not.\n[doctor] okay. i\'ll be back in a few minutes. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] all right so kyle morales is a pleasant fifty eight year old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain\n[doctor] on onset of his pain began two weeks ago when he was lifting a box of books and felt a pop the pain is described as aching and sharp his pain has been increasing since the time of onset and is beginning to radiate down his left leg the patient denies any pain with sitting he adds that he has a sedentary job the patient states that his pain does not affect his job however he feels that he does not want to be as active as he was previously he tries to exercise a few times per week however he is inconsistent he denies any numbness tingling loss of sensation weakness and loss of control of his bladder or bowels\n[doctor] the patient has been taking ibuprofen and tylenol for pain which provides some relief he has not been evaluated for surgery\n[doctor] social history he denies smoking the patient has a history of playing soccer in the midfield position\n[doctor] family history the patient states 